WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.497
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.010
I don't comment anything.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.110
I do not leave comments.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.960
I'm totally lazy about it.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.780
And I'm sorry everyone
who has to read my code,

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.650
because it is completely
wild west, on your own,

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.220
figure it out from
the code, too bad.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.050
I comment nothing.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.780
The code is the comment.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.280
Yeah.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.391
Writing too many comments.

00:00:35.391 --> 00:00:38.260
Too many comments?

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.220
Well, I could reduce
the verbosity.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.190
I'm still trying to
learn, but because I

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:47.640
like to go back to
my code oftentimes I

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.720
feel like it's just like if
you come back to your code

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.460
a couple of months
later you'll probably

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.180
have no idea what you did.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:00.510
But I like to kind of think
about that and mitigating

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.250
that problem in
the future and try

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.560
to write some comments
and documentation.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.980
Sometimes the variable
names I use have no meaning,

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.150
they're just placeholders.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:13.152
And that's OK, but it
helps when you're actually

00:01:13.152 --> 00:01:15.360
deploying something or you
have something that you're

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.735
trying to share
with other people

00:01:16.735 --> 00:01:20.570
to have something that has
meaning instead of just

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:26.460
a T or an X. I also am
really, really bad about going

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.360
back and cleaning up my code.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.730
It's a bad habit
generally in research.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:35.430
My worst programming
hack or habit

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:40.140
is to always take someone's
like a research code base

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.620
and start cleaning it up
and try to make it like--

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.570
remove all of the
boilerplate code

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:50.140
for like check pointing, like
parameter management and all

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:50.640
of those.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.810
And basically plug-in all of my
own like open source packages

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.490
because it slows
me down initially.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.890
But I think like it does
make like a clean code

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.200
base is important for
moving really quickly

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.960
and for like
communicating clearly

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.020
with their collaborators
and with the others working

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:07.800
with your code.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.949
I really dislike when
stuff is not structured,

00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:13.050
but when code is
not structured well.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:14.880
But then obviously when
you're sort of just

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.213
starting on something there
is always that temptation to

00:02:17.213 --> 00:02:19.560
or there's this trade
between spending

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.620
too much time structuring things
versus just getting it working.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:26.130
And so I definitely have
found that pet peeve just

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:27.962
by doing it myself
and being frustrated

00:02:27.962 --> 00:02:28.920
at myself looking back.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.530
You know, why did I not sort
of organize this better?

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.360
And now I'm sort of
wasting time trying

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.780
to figure out what it was
I was doing a year ago.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.180
Not going to sleep
when I should.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.200
So problems seem to become
just like astronomically more

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.660
difficult as the day wears on.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.270
And I think that
really the answer

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:52.890
is to go home at 5 o'clock or
6 o'clock and forget about it

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:55.110
and look at it again
the next morning.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:58.820
But like all of us, I'm
a busy grad student.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:02:59.820
I want to make progress.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.980
Sometimes I get excited
about what I'm building

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.620
and so I'll stay up
super late working on it.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.020
And I'll typically
find that those six

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:11.190
hours that I might spend in
the evening are terrible.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.110
I introduce a ton of
bugs into my code.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:16.080
I find like problems
just insurmountable.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.815
And then I'll go to sleep and
be like, oh, nothing works.

00:03:18.815 --> 00:03:20.190
And I'll wake up
the next morning

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.070
and fix it in 10 minutes.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:25.210
So trying not to do that.

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:27.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

